WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.465
I'd say more importantly for us is actually about the EV transition because EVs today, 

00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:12.884
the uptake is happening, but they are a luxury item.

00:00:19.436 --> 00:00:21.980
- So we've had a lot of
talk over the last few days

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.565
about the economy.

00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:27.527
You have a very interesting lens

00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:29.946
into the economy through
the ride share business

00:00:29.946 --> 00:00:31.489
and the eats business.

00:00:31.489 --> 00:00:33.616
Can you give a sense of
starting with the US,

00:00:33.616 --> 00:00:36.077
what are you seeing actually happening

00:00:36.077 --> 00:00:37.954
with consumer behavior?

00:00:37.954 --> 00:00:42.834
- So the consumer from our
standpoint, remains quite strong.

00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:46.880
Now, the lens with which
we're observing consumer spend

00:00:47.797 --> 00:00:51.885
is obviously spend on mobility,
spend on delivery of food,

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:53.470
groceries, et cetera.

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:56.014
And I think that one of the tailwinds

00:00:56.014 --> 00:00:58.725
that we are experiencing

00:00:58.725 --> 00:01:03.730
is because of the switch
from spend on retail

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:08.985
that was happening when
everyone was at home

00:01:08.985 --> 00:01:12.447
and going crazy and just
ordering her a bunch of stuff,

00:01:12.447 --> 00:01:14.199
back to spend on services.

00:01:14.199 --> 00:01:16.993
As the world is reopening,
people are going out again,

00:01:16.993 --> 00:01:19.370
they're going to see
friends, families, et cetera.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:21.915
I'd argue that's a little
more healthy spend,

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:23.583
but we can have a debate
about that or not.

00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:28.588
And as a result of that
shift back to services spend,

00:01:30.215 --> 00:01:32.717
the growth in our business

00:01:32.717 --> 00:01:35.637
and what we are seeing in
terms of consumer spend

00:01:35.637 --> 00:01:38.973
on a local basis is actually quite strong.

00:01:38.973 --> 00:01:41.935
So we will NSR Q4 earnings,

00:01:41.935 --> 00:01:44.604
but the range of bookings
growth that we got to

00:01:44.604 --> 00:01:48.108
was 23 to 27%, that's quite robust.

00:01:48.108 --> 00:01:51.194
And that spend is strong both
the mobility and delivery.

00:01:51.194 --> 00:01:54.948
So it's not in one segment or the other.

00:01:56.157 --> 00:01:59.244
We've been obviously because
of all prophecies of doom

00:01:59.244 --> 00:02:01.037
coming up, et cetera,

00:02:01.037 --> 00:02:03.289
taking a very careful look in
terms of our best business.

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:05.667
Can we segment it one way or the other?

00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:08.461
One of the areas that
we've been focused on

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:11.005
is the effective
Inflation on the business.

00:02:11.005 --> 00:02:12.715
And the effect is twofold.

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:16.845
One is we're seeing our
restaurant partners now

00:02:16.845 --> 00:02:20.431
pass through increases in their cost,

00:02:20.431 --> 00:02:22.600
service costs, food costs, et cetera.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:26.980
So menu Inflation is up about eight or 9%

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:30.483
and that does have an effect
on unit volume, right?

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:34.237
The classic economics apply,

00:02:34.237 --> 00:02:37.407
which is unit volume than is a bit slower.

00:02:37.407 --> 00:02:38.867
So for example,

00:02:38.867 --> 00:02:41.744
unit volume in our delivery
businesses in the single digits,

00:02:41.744 --> 00:02:43.371
decent but single digits,

00:02:43.371 --> 00:02:46.207
mobility business, it's double digits.

00:02:46.207 --> 00:02:47.834
The other factor is-

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:49.460
- And are people ordering
like cheaper dishes

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:52.130
and less sided dishes and this?

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:53.173
- Not really.
- Okay.

00:02:53.173 --> 00:02:56.426
- We were looking for trade down behavior

00:02:56.426 --> 00:02:57.427
one way or the other,

00:02:57.427 --> 00:03:01.014
but it's resulting in just
average basket sizes being up.

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:04.100
And unit volume not
growing quite as robustly,

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:05.602
again, single digits, which is fine.

00:03:05.602 --> 00:03:07.645
Versus the mobility businesses,

00:03:07.645 --> 00:03:11.274
which is growing in the very,
very solid double digits.

00:03:11.274 --> 00:03:12.984
The other factor that we're seeing

00:03:12.984 --> 00:03:16.863
as it relates to Inflation
is that about 80% of earners

00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:19.324
who are coming onto the platform platform,

00:03:19.324 --> 00:03:22.535
there are about 5 million
people earning on Uber

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:24.204
on a global basis.

00:03:24.204 --> 00:03:27.207
80% of those who are signing up

00:03:27.207 --> 00:03:29.626
are seeing that Inflation cost of living

00:03:29.626 --> 00:03:32.921
had something to do with
their decision to sign up,

00:03:32.921 --> 00:03:36.424
pay for their groceries, pay
for their expenses, et cetera.

00:03:36.424 --> 00:03:39.177
So in that way are being a platform

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:42.680
that can take a little bit of a shock,

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:47.477
that real folks are experiencing
in terms of Inflation

00:03:47.477 --> 00:03:49.229
that makes a platform more attractive.

00:03:49.229 --> 00:03:51.147
And to some extent, that's a service

00:03:51.147 --> 00:03:54.525
that we are serving in terms of society.

00:03:54.525 --> 00:03:57.403
- And are you seeing, just quickly,

00:03:57.403 --> 00:03:58.821
are you seeing the same in Europe?

00:03:58.821 --> 00:04:00.698
- Does quickly mean, go faster?

00:04:00.698 --> 00:04:02.659
- No, I don't mean that
you were verbose at all.

00:04:02.659 --> 00:04:04.786
I thought it was a very
interesting answer.

00:04:04.786 --> 00:04:07.664
I don't wanna spend too
long on the economy.

00:04:07.664 --> 00:04:08.498
I wanna get into the business stuff.

00:04:08.498 --> 00:04:09.666
- Of course.

00:04:09.666 --> 00:04:12.377
- Europe, is that the same picture?

00:04:12.377 --> 00:04:13.670
- Very similar.
- Very similar.

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:16.506
- The picture in Europe
looks quite similar.

00:04:16.506 --> 00:04:18.007
Business is strong.

00:04:18.007 --> 00:04:18.841
Same thing in Latin America.

00:04:18.841 --> 00:04:23.096
So I think the shift
from retail to services

00:04:23.096 --> 00:04:24.639
is pretty consistent.

00:04:24.639 --> 00:04:27.225
I think your headline or
headline that I read was

00:04:27.225 --> 00:04:31.729
that retail spend, looked
pretty weak recently.

00:04:31.729 --> 00:04:35.441
And again, I wouldn't
necessarily take long-term trends

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:39.070
out of that because we made
the mistake during the pandemic

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:41.406
of thinking that the retail boom was,

00:04:41.406 --> 00:04:44.158
long term trend turned out that,

00:04:44.158 --> 00:04:47.328
everything kind of returns to norm.

00:04:47.328 --> 00:04:51.040
So I do think that later in
the second half of the year,

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:55.003
the growth should modulate
off of a very, very high base.

00:04:55.003 --> 00:04:57.338
As services spend returns to normal

00:04:57.338 --> 00:04:59.340
and then we'll see what happens with-

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:01.092
- But presumably given
the lack of experience.

00:05:01.092 --> 00:05:02.719
You don't really know
to what extent this is

00:05:02.719 --> 00:05:04.053
just resilience of your business

00:05:04.053 --> 00:05:07.307
versus what the economy's doing.

00:05:07.307 --> 00:05:10.893
- I've seen many, many times CEOs

00:05:10.893 --> 00:05:13.187
have made predictions
based on short-term trends

00:05:13.187 --> 00:05:14.981
and declare them as long-term trends.

00:05:14.981 --> 00:05:17.984
Reversion to norm is a
very, very powerful force.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:21.112
- So you wrote a memo in May

00:05:21.946 --> 00:05:23.489
about being hardcore on costs

00:05:23.489 --> 00:05:25.408
and seeing hiring as a privilege

00:05:25.408 --> 00:05:26.909
and pushing for profitability.

00:05:27.744 --> 00:05:29.579
Can you talk just very quickly
about the impact of that

00:05:29.579 --> 00:05:30.413
on the business?

00:05:30.413 --> 00:05:33.750
I mean, how has that sort
of galvanized people?

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:35.043
And then I gotta follow up.

00:05:35.043 --> 00:05:36.627
- Absolutely.

00:05:36.627 --> 00:05:38.379
We were pretty early in making that call,

00:05:38.379 --> 00:05:40.465
thank God, in hindsight.

00:05:41.924 --> 00:05:44.886
I saw things changing and in business,

00:05:44.886 --> 00:05:47.930
the biggest mistakes that
I see executives make is

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:50.808
when they don't know what
the definition of good is.

00:05:50.808 --> 00:05:54.062
They think they're doing a
good job, but they actually,

00:05:54.062 --> 00:05:55.938
they're aiming for a target,

00:05:55.938 --> 00:05:57.565
but it's the wrong target.

00:05:58.649 --> 00:06:01.110
And the reason that I wrote that memo was

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:04.864
because I started seeing
what we're seeing right now,

00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:06.407
which is interest rates coming up,

00:06:06.407 --> 00:06:08.409
capital being pulled outta the market

00:06:08.409 --> 00:06:12.830
and performance that was defined
as excellence in the past.

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:15.166
Lots of growth, who cares about profit

00:06:15.166 --> 00:06:18.669
is performance that is described

00:06:18.669 --> 00:06:21.756
as not good enough or
middling in our new world.

00:06:21.756 --> 00:06:23.257
So I didn't want to,

00:06:23.257 --> 00:06:24.926
and we're a big organization,

00:06:24.926 --> 00:06:26.427
we organized behind plans,

00:06:26.427 --> 00:06:28.304
lots of people working their asses off

00:06:28.304 --> 00:06:30.139
to get to those plans.

00:06:30.139 --> 00:06:31.766
And so the reason why
I wrote the memo was,

00:06:31.766 --> 00:06:33.476
we are just like any company,

00:06:33.476 --> 00:06:36.979
we had annual plans in place
and totally targets, et cetera.

00:06:36.979 --> 00:06:39.565
And everyone is kind of
gunning hard for these targets.

00:06:39.565 --> 00:06:43.069
And I felt like our definition of good

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:44.195
in terms of performance,

00:06:44.195 --> 00:06:46.114
in terms of what our own plans were,

00:06:46.114 --> 00:06:47.865
were no longer good enough.

00:06:47.865 --> 00:06:50.868
And that we had to be
much tougher on costs

00:06:50.868 --> 00:06:52.703
and we had to achieve
the same growth plans

00:06:52.703 --> 00:06:54.163
with a lot less investment

00:06:54.163 --> 00:06:56.124
because the world was changing,

00:06:56.124 --> 00:06:58.543
investor expectations were changing,

00:06:58.543 --> 00:07:01.129
as a result our expectations
would would change.

00:07:01.129 --> 00:07:03.256
So honestly, the early reaction

00:07:03.256 --> 00:07:07.260
like that wasn't a wonderful memo

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:10.221
to get from your CEO on a Sunday night.

00:07:10.221 --> 00:07:15.226
So it kind of landed a bit
like a lead balloon, initially.

00:07:16.144 --> 00:07:19.689
But we as a company, as a
culture that really rallies,

00:07:19.689 --> 00:07:22.316
we talk to our teams,
the teams really rallied.

00:07:22.316 --> 00:07:24.652
And I think our performance since then,

00:07:24.652 --> 00:07:29.365
has been on an operating
basis really leading the pack.

00:07:29.365 --> 00:07:32.118
And I think as we enter 2023

00:07:32.118 --> 00:07:35.079
at least I feel we are well prepared

00:07:35.079 --> 00:07:36.372
because we've been operating

00:07:36.372 --> 00:07:38.708
based on the expectations
of the real world

00:07:38.708 --> 00:07:40.668
for a longer time than
a lot of other companies

00:07:40.668 --> 00:07:42.378
that you see are making adjustments now.

00:07:42.378 --> 00:07:43.880
- And a lot of companies to that point

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:45.631
are making adjustments.

00:07:45.631 --> 00:07:48.843
And having to lay people
off insignificant numbers.

00:07:48.843 --> 00:07:51.304
Is your early action meant
you are gonna avoid that?

00:07:51.304 --> 00:07:53.055
I mean, how do you see that situation?

00:07:53.055 --> 00:07:54.223
- I certainly hope so, right.

00:07:54.223 --> 00:07:57.435
We're not at this point planning on any

00:07:57.435 --> 00:07:59.645
company-wide layoffs, et cetera.

00:07:59.645 --> 00:08:03.316
It's a job of the CEO
to performance manage

00:08:03.316 --> 00:08:06.319
people and parts of the
company all the time.

00:08:06.319 --> 00:08:08.154
I'm always gonna be taking people out,

00:08:08.154 --> 00:08:11.282
if there's a group or division
that's underperforming,

00:08:11.282 --> 00:08:13.784
take people out and then if
I should reinvest those folks

00:08:13.784 --> 00:08:16.412
or reinvest assets or marketing monies,

00:08:16.412 --> 00:08:20.333
that's part of the everyday job of a CEO.

00:08:20.333 --> 00:08:22.543
So we will be making
those kinds of shifts.

00:08:22.543 --> 00:08:27.548
But the core business and the
core workforce, et cetera,

00:08:27.632 --> 00:08:29.509
we think are well-balanced

00:08:29.509 --> 00:08:31.385
and well positioned for the future.

00:08:31.385 --> 00:08:33.638
I certainly hope it stays that way,

00:08:33.638 --> 00:08:35.348
but it is a dynamic environment

00:08:35.348 --> 00:08:37.517
and we're gonna adjust pretty quickly.

00:08:37.517 --> 00:08:41.479
- So very broadly, the end
of the cheap money era,

00:08:41.479 --> 00:08:43.523
you've talked about Inflation
and rising interest rates

00:08:43.523 --> 00:08:44.899
has obviously had this massive impact

00:08:44.899 --> 00:08:47.026
on Tech valuations and Tech in general.

00:08:48.277 --> 00:08:50.863
How far do you think we are
through that adjustment?

00:08:52.365 --> 00:08:57.370
- I think that the 80%
of the shock is behind us

00:09:00.581 --> 00:09:02.166
and I think a lot of
companies you're seeing

00:09:02.166 --> 00:09:06.128
are making that initial
adjustment to get there.

00:09:06.128 --> 00:09:08.839
I personally think that

00:09:08.839 --> 00:09:13.803
the markets generally are betting
on a softer fed than I see

00:09:16.722 --> 00:09:19.475
while commodity price,
Inflation, et cetera

00:09:19.475 --> 00:09:21.310
is definitely easing up.

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:23.813
We have a freight business

00:09:23.813 --> 00:09:25.773
that's involved in transportation.

00:09:25.773 --> 00:09:28.693
Transportation costs are coming
down pretty significantly.

00:09:28.693 --> 00:09:33.698
I don't see service
Inflation as being transitory

00:09:33.781 --> 00:09:37.743
and I think that it's making
its way through the system.

00:09:37.743 --> 00:09:40.871
There's a delayed effect in
terms of Inflationary effects

00:09:40.871 --> 00:09:44.625
through the supply chain then
all the way up to consumer

00:09:44.625 --> 00:09:48.087
and Inflation moves
slowly through the system.

00:09:48.087 --> 00:09:49.380
So we saw it, we were surprised by it

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:51.841
because we didn't see
it in the end product.

00:09:51.841 --> 00:09:54.885
And so I think that, at
least from my standpoint,

00:09:55.720 --> 00:10:00.099
I think the Fed's gonna
stay higher for longer.

00:10:00.099 --> 00:10:04.103
So for companies that
are hoping for a reprieve

00:10:04.103 --> 00:10:05.396
or companies that are hoping,

00:10:05.396 --> 00:10:06.439
"Hey, this will be a year,

00:10:06.439 --> 00:10:07.690
things will be better next year.

00:10:07.690 --> 00:10:09.984
I just have to like make
it through this year,"

00:10:09.984 --> 00:10:11.569
I think they're gonna be disappointed.

00:10:11.569 --> 00:10:15.156
- So for those of us who aren't
sitting in Silicon Valley,

00:10:15.156 --> 00:10:17.867
under the surface, how bad is the pain

00:10:17.867 --> 00:10:20.411
and what are you seeing
in terms of the people,

00:10:20.411 --> 00:10:23.122
your suppliers, people
you do business with?

00:10:23.122 --> 00:10:26.375
- First of all, I think
it's a healthy shift

00:10:26.375 --> 00:10:27.710
that we're going through, right?

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:30.671
Blue collar worker paid generally

00:10:30.671 --> 00:10:33.257
his trailed GDP growth, et cetera.

00:10:33.257 --> 00:10:35.760
So I think the shift
that we're going through

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:38.929
in terms of services. industries,

00:10:38.929 --> 00:10:42.558
Uber drivers, couriers, et cetera,

00:10:42.558 --> 00:10:47.063
earnings moving up at strong rates,

00:10:47.063 --> 00:10:50.566
I think that's a healthy
shift for economy.

00:10:50.566 --> 00:10:53.819
And yes, our economy's gonna
have to make an adjustment,

00:10:53.819 --> 00:10:56.656
but I think it'll be a healthy adjustment

00:10:56.656 --> 00:10:58.407
one way or the other.

00:10:58.407 --> 00:11:00.576
- Your competitors had a rougher ride,

00:11:00.576 --> 00:11:02.286
your main competitor.

00:11:02.286 --> 00:11:03.120
- That's fair.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:06.457
- Is there anyone that you
particularly would fear

00:11:06.457 --> 00:11:09.251
in a consolidation type
situation coming into your market

00:11:09.251 --> 00:11:10.419
as a competitor?

00:11:10.419 --> 00:11:12.088
I mean, who do you kind of-

00:11:12.088 --> 00:11:13.381
- Fear in terms of ride share?

00:11:13.381 --> 00:11:14.757
- Of coming in and buying Lyft

00:11:14.757 --> 00:11:15.841
and actually doing something with it?

00:11:15.841 --> 00:11:18.678
Are there any of the big Tech operators

00:11:18.678 --> 00:11:20.471
or people that you particularly fear

00:11:20.471 --> 00:11:21.847
coming in and taking you on?

00:11:21.847 --> 00:11:23.099
- Honestly, thorough,

00:11:23.099 --> 00:11:24.725
I don't spend too much
time thinking about it

00:11:24.725 --> 00:11:26.560
because it's a waste of time
'cause I'll get it wrong.

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:27.603
- Fine, okay.

00:11:27.603 --> 00:11:28.938
So let's move on from that.

00:11:28.938 --> 00:11:32.441
So I wanna do a quick
fire round of questions

00:11:32.441 --> 00:11:34.985
and then we'll go on to some other stuff.

00:11:34.985 --> 00:11:37.154
When do you expect a meaningful number

00:11:37.154 --> 00:11:39.532
of self-driving cars in your fleet?

00:11:39.532 --> 00:11:40.950
- Define meaningful?

00:11:43.035 --> 00:11:44.286
- 20%.

00:11:44.286 --> 00:11:45.121
- Oh wow.

00:11:48.124 --> 00:11:49.750
12 to 15 years.

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:50.918
- 12 to 15 years.

00:11:50.918 --> 00:11:53.295
- My sec-
- Please don't remind me.

00:11:53.295 --> 00:11:54.338
12 to 15 years from now.

00:11:55.339 --> 00:11:57.341
- But it seems to push out each time

00:11:57.341 --> 00:11:59.260
someone gets asked that
question, but okay.

00:11:59.260 --> 00:12:01.554
- Okay 15 to 20.

00:12:01.554 --> 00:12:02.930
(audience laughs)

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:04.390
- Will it be a US recession?

00:12:05.349 --> 00:12:06.684
- Yes.

00:12:06.684 --> 00:12:07.727
- Okay.

00:12:07.727 --> 00:12:11.147
Will NASDAQ be higher or
lower at the end of the year?

00:12:11.147 --> 00:12:12.732
- I don't care.

00:12:12.732 --> 00:12:13.858
- What is the biggest benefit

00:12:13.858 --> 00:12:15.651
of having people back in the office?

00:12:16.944 --> 00:12:19.280
- This, the reaction.

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:22.450
I think being back in the
office is incredibly important.

00:12:22.450 --> 00:12:25.411
I think that hybrid is the better way,

00:12:25.411 --> 00:12:27.830
but we've got to make
that human connection,

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:28.873
I missed it desperately.

00:12:28.873 --> 00:12:31.167
So I'm trying to force
our team to come back

00:12:31.167 --> 00:12:34.754
and some of them are happy about
it and some of them aren't.

00:12:34.754 --> 00:12:37.840
- Is there a drawback of having
people back in the office?

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:39.216
- Sure, convenience,

00:12:39.216 --> 00:12:41.927
but some parts of work aren't convenient.

00:12:41.927 --> 00:12:42.887
That's why it's called work.

00:12:42.887 --> 00:12:44.638
- Okay, and in terms of,

00:12:46.223 --> 00:12:48.225
you get all of the benefit
of everyone meeting up

00:12:48.225 --> 00:12:49.393
at the water cooler and chatting,

00:12:49.393 --> 00:12:53.189
is there a downside to
people spending too much time

00:12:53.189 --> 00:12:55.232
doing stuff that maybe is not so relevant?

00:12:55.232 --> 00:12:56.650
Or is that just the positive of thing.

00:12:56.650 --> 00:12:58.986
- No, I think it's part
of being human, right?

00:12:58.986 --> 00:13:03.073
I actually think you
hit a nail on the head,

00:13:03.073 --> 00:13:06.535
which is Zoom life is
very transactional, right?

00:13:06.535 --> 00:13:09.580
It's everything is by appointment,

00:13:09.580 --> 00:13:12.708
you start at a certain time,
you end at a certain time.

00:13:12.708 --> 00:13:14.543
You can't have any casual encounters.

00:13:14.543 --> 00:13:16.420
Like think about Davos,

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:17.838
like imagine having
Davos over Zoom, right?

00:13:17.838 --> 00:13:20.424
It would be terrible.

00:13:20.424 --> 00:13:21.801
And so-
- [Thorold] We did.

00:13:21.801 --> 00:13:24.345
- This is, yeah, how was it?

00:13:24.345 --> 00:13:27.723
- I don't think I should comment
here, but we'll chat later.

00:13:27.723 --> 00:13:28.933
Yeah, I mean it was different.

00:13:28.933 --> 00:13:30.184
- So it's just different.

00:13:31.227 --> 00:13:34.063
The casual encounters and
things get done faster.

00:13:34.063 --> 00:13:35.981
I really don't believe it
should be the only way.

00:13:35.981 --> 00:13:38.275
So I've heard some CEOs who are like,

00:13:38.275 --> 00:13:39.193
"Five days a week we're going back."

00:13:39.193 --> 00:13:41.570
I don't think going back is the right way,

00:13:41.570 --> 00:13:44.657
but I actually think
the hybrid of the two,

00:13:44.657 --> 00:13:47.284
you get the energy in terms
of being with your team

00:13:47.284 --> 00:13:50.120
and then being remote
for a week, totally fine.

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:52.790
But then you need that
connection and energy

00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:56.752
and kind of the quick
interactions to get things done.

00:13:56.752 --> 00:13:57.837
- And just final thing,

00:13:57.837 --> 00:14:02.842
are you still planning a Uber
specifically designed car?

00:14:03.467 --> 00:14:08.472
- We are working with various OEMs

00:14:08.639 --> 00:14:12.768
to design cars for ride share.

00:14:12.768 --> 00:14:15.729
We are by far the biggest
ride share partner out there.

00:14:15.729 --> 00:14:16.981
So I think we will be the first partner

00:14:16.981 --> 00:14:19.108
for whom they design, but I'd expect them,

00:14:19.108 --> 00:14:22.152
it's in their interest to
leverage whatever platforms

00:14:22.152 --> 00:14:27.157
that they build across a
maximum volume of rides.

00:14:27.366 --> 00:14:29.201
But we're definitely quite interested

00:14:30.244 --> 00:14:32.663
in talking OEMs a about
purpose built vehicles.

00:14:32.663 --> 00:14:35.040
I'd say more importantly for us is

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:37.668
actually about the EV transition

00:14:37.668 --> 00:14:42.673
because EVs today, the
uptake is happening,

00:14:43.424 --> 00:14:45.968
but they are luxury item.

00:14:45.968 --> 00:14:50.973
The most popular car in
the Uber fleet is a Prius.

00:14:55.519 --> 00:14:57.855
Affordable, fuel efficient.

00:14:57.855 --> 00:15:02.234
And so we need more affordable
Electric Vehicles out there.

00:15:02.234 --> 00:15:05.529
That is the headline conversation
that I'm having with OEMs.

00:15:05.529 --> 00:15:07.156
Purposeful vehicles are great.

00:15:07.156 --> 00:15:08.741
- Okay.

00:15:08.741 --> 00:15:10.367
You touched on drivers.

00:15:10.367 --> 00:15:12.703
Is the shortage over, I mean,

00:15:12.703 --> 00:15:14.538
there's obviously a lot of
talk about the tightness

00:15:14.538 --> 00:15:15.831
in the Labor market
over the last few years,

00:15:15.831 --> 00:15:17.499
which again was Inflationary.

00:15:17.499 --> 00:15:19.126
You talked about the wages
coming up at the bottom,

00:15:19.126 --> 00:15:20.210
which is a positive.

00:15:21.378 --> 00:15:23.172
Has that mitigated significantly?

00:15:23.172 --> 00:15:24.465
Are you still struggling to find drivers

00:15:24.465 --> 00:15:25.549
or is that over now?

00:15:25.549 --> 00:15:27.217
- The shortage isn't over,

00:15:27.217 --> 00:15:31.722
but the momentum for us is
moving in the right direction.

00:15:31.722 --> 00:15:34.808
We made a pretty fundamental pivot

00:15:34.808 --> 00:15:39.813
in terms of our company and
our efforts to be earner first,

00:15:40.064 --> 00:15:43.901
to be driver first, to really
focus on the driver experience

00:15:43.901 --> 00:15:47.529
in terms of onboarding,
improving customer service.

00:15:47.529 --> 00:15:49.782
Driver earnings are excellent,

00:15:49.782 --> 00:15:51.533
but even giving them
visibility, for example,

00:15:51.533 --> 00:15:55.245
to earnings drivers in the
US and many other markets

00:15:55.245 --> 00:15:58.832
didn't know what destination
the rider was going to,

00:15:58.832 --> 00:16:00.834
we now give them upfront destinations.

00:16:00.834 --> 00:16:02.002
So it's not just about pay,

00:16:02.002 --> 00:16:05.297
it's about the entire driver experience

00:16:05.297 --> 00:16:10.260
that's resulted in driver
turnover being down 20%.

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:12.596
Drivers are driving more.

00:16:12.596 --> 00:16:16.809
So driver engagement is up in the teens

00:16:16.809 --> 00:16:19.728
we added about a million drivers.

00:16:19.728 --> 00:16:22.064
So we're up to actually a million earners,

00:16:22.064 --> 00:16:23.107
drivers and courier.

00:16:23.107 --> 00:16:26.485
So we're up to 5 million
earners now on a global basis.

00:16:26.485 --> 00:16:30.280
We're the world's largest source
of work, not just gig work.

00:16:30.280 --> 00:16:34.493
And the momentum in terms of
our earner base is growing

00:16:34.493 --> 00:16:36.537
because the experience is getting better.

00:16:36.537 --> 00:16:39.331
And certainly the experience on Uber

00:16:39.331 --> 00:16:43.293
versus our competition is
starting to create some space.

00:16:43.293 --> 00:16:46.755
- And Inflation in terms of
the wages they need to earn.

00:16:46.755 --> 00:16:48.549
Is that still very high.

00:16:49.425 --> 00:16:53.971
- Driver earnings versus
driver earnings last year

00:16:53.971 --> 00:16:55.848
significantly increased?

00:16:55.848 --> 00:16:57.474
I think that the increases this year

00:16:57.474 --> 00:16:59.059
are gonna mitigate someone,

00:16:59.059 --> 00:17:01.937
but I do think drivers are gonna
earn more this coming year.

00:17:01.937 --> 00:17:03.272
On average.
- Okay.

00:17:03.272 --> 00:17:05.232
And to what extent does that, and again,

00:17:05.232 --> 00:17:07.109
so this is a a a little pet peeve of mind,

00:17:07.109 --> 00:17:09.903
but to what extent has that
better visibility for drivers

00:17:09.903 --> 00:17:12.489
hurt the consumer experience
in terms of cancellations

00:17:12.489 --> 00:17:15.284
and availability of rides?

00:17:15.284 --> 00:17:17.411
has that affected the consumer appearance?

00:17:17.411 --> 00:17:21.665
- No, it has and should
improving cancellations in that,

00:17:21.665 --> 00:17:23.375
in the previous world,

00:17:23.375 --> 00:17:26.211
drivers who didn't see the destination,

00:17:26.211 --> 00:17:28.630
but then once they accepted the trip,

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:31.633
saw a destination that they didn't like,

00:17:31.633 --> 00:17:33.886
they would cancel on you.

00:17:33.886 --> 00:17:36.764
Now what we do is we
show them the destination

00:17:36.764 --> 00:17:39.308
so that they already know
when they accept a trip.

00:17:39.308 --> 00:17:41.769
So the cancellation is
from traffic, et cetera.

00:17:41.769 --> 00:17:43.937
Now sometimes the result is,

00:17:43.937 --> 00:17:46.982
and the experience is, the spinner time,

00:17:46.982 --> 00:17:49.526
which is amount of time you
say, "I wanna get a driver."

00:17:49.526 --> 00:17:51.528
And then when we match
you up with a driver,

00:17:51.528 --> 00:17:53.447
that may take a little longer

00:17:53.447 --> 00:17:57.076
and that's because the offer
may go to you, it may go to me,

00:17:57.076 --> 00:18:00.704
it may go to a third driver
before that driver accepts,

00:18:00.704 --> 00:18:03.499
but they're accepting with
a lot more information.

00:18:03.499 --> 00:18:04.625
We think that's a win.

00:18:05.834 --> 00:18:06.752
- What would it mean,

00:18:06.752 --> 00:18:09.254
there's been a lot of talk around whether

00:18:09.254 --> 00:18:10.089
Uber driver are employees

00:18:10.089 --> 00:18:12.716
or should be employees
in certain countries.

00:18:12.716 --> 00:18:15.052
What impact would it have on the business

00:18:15.052 --> 00:18:17.179
if you had to make everyone an employee?

00:18:17.179 --> 00:18:20.474
- Oh, it would be
terrible for the business,

00:18:20.474 --> 00:18:21.558
would be terrible for drivers.

00:18:21.558 --> 00:18:24.478
I think the most important
thing to understand

00:18:24.478 --> 00:18:26.105
in terms of drivers is,

00:18:26.105 --> 00:18:29.775
I was in Amsterdam before I came here,

00:18:29.775 --> 00:18:31.610
94% of drivers in Amsterdam

00:18:31.610 --> 00:18:34.196
have said that they don't
want to be employees,

00:18:34.196 --> 00:18:37.116
in the US it's 80% of drivers
don't want to be employees.

00:18:37.116 --> 00:18:39.118
If you want a job in this economy,

00:18:39.118 --> 00:18:41.954
almost anywhere you
are, you can get a job.

00:18:41.954 --> 00:18:44.414
So there's a group of people
who are self-selecting

00:18:44.414 --> 00:18:46.500
into earnings opportunities on Uber

00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:49.253
and or other gig economy companies

00:18:49.253 --> 00:18:52.840
that want flexible and now increasingly

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:55.092
very strong earnings opportunities, right?

00:18:55.092 --> 00:18:57.803
In the US average earnings
per utilized hours,

00:18:57.803 --> 00:18:59.471
39 bucks an hour,

00:18:59.471 --> 00:19:03.559
we are matching up the flexibility

00:19:03.559 --> 00:19:06.436
of those earnings
opportunities increasingly now

00:19:06.436 --> 00:19:08.105
with benefits,

00:19:08.105 --> 00:19:10.023
in France, for example,

00:19:10.023 --> 00:19:11.525
as a result of sectoral bargaining

00:19:11.525 --> 00:19:14.111
representation by the drivers,

00:19:14.111 --> 00:19:17.239
the minimum amount that a
driver can make on a short trip

00:19:17.239 --> 00:19:22.202
is seven Euro 65, in the UK
they've got vacation pay,

00:19:23.453 --> 00:19:25.038
pension benefits, et cetera.

00:19:25.038 --> 00:19:27.624
We're having discussions with states,

00:19:27.624 --> 00:19:29.084
governments, et cetera,

00:19:29.084 --> 00:19:32.796
as to what those benefits can be

00:19:32.796 --> 00:19:36.133
so that you now have an outcome,

00:19:36.133 --> 00:19:39.928
which is the flexibility to be
able to work where you want,

00:19:39.928 --> 00:19:42.681
when you want minimum earnings standards-

00:19:42.681 --> 00:19:45.642
- It's quite a gray area
between employee and contractor.

00:19:45.642 --> 00:19:47.811
There's a lot of intermediate-

00:19:47.811 --> 00:19:51.148
- Yeah, I'd say it's
a good, it's a hybrid,

00:19:51.148 --> 00:19:53.859
that's better than where we came from-

00:19:53.859 --> 00:19:55.944
- And this is based on what drivers want

00:19:55.944 --> 00:19:57.196
and what makes sense for you.

00:19:57.196 --> 00:19:58.530
You're getting the equilibrium.

00:19:58.530 --> 00:20:01.158
- Very much, to fully
answer your question,

00:20:01.158 --> 00:20:05.078
in markets where we have
flipped over to employment,

00:20:05.078 --> 00:20:08.832
because we can operate Uber
in an employment model,

00:20:08.832 --> 00:20:11.210
about two-thirds of drivers

00:20:11.210 --> 00:20:13.921
essentially lose the opportunity
to work on the platform.

00:20:13.921 --> 00:20:15.380
That's a big number.

00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:16.548
That will be a tragedy

00:20:16.548 --> 00:20:19.509
when you're talking about 5
million earners on the platform.

00:20:19.509 --> 00:20:20.344
- Is there ever gonna be,

00:20:20.344 --> 00:20:23.639
I mean a lot of companies
have this a situation

00:20:23.639 --> 00:20:25.390
where there's the American dream

00:20:25.390 --> 00:20:27.100
of you start on the shop floor
and you end up as the CEO.

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:30.020
Is there ever gonna be a
situation with Uber drivers

00:20:30.020 --> 00:20:32.272
where that career progression

00:20:32.272 --> 00:20:33.106
inside the company is possible?

00:20:33.106 --> 00:20:35.192
Or is it always gonna
be drivers and the rest?

00:20:35.192 --> 00:20:37.819
- Yeah, that situation is now, so one is,

00:20:37.819 --> 00:20:40.155
you should keep in mind that drivers,

00:20:40.155 --> 00:20:43.450
the longer drivers drive
the more money they make

00:20:43.450 --> 00:20:45.035
because they understand the system,

00:20:45.035 --> 00:20:46.411
they understand, "When should I drive,

00:20:46.411 --> 00:20:48.914
when should I not drive,
where should I go, et cetera."

00:20:48.914 --> 00:20:51.124
And as they learn the
system, they just earn more.

00:20:51.124 --> 00:20:53.418
It's a natural learning process.

00:20:53.418 --> 00:20:56.171
The amount drivers make
is directly related

00:20:56.171 --> 00:20:58.215
to their utilization on the platform

00:20:58.215 --> 00:21:00.801
and the utilization on the
platform gets better and better

00:21:00.801 --> 00:21:02.302
as you understand the platform.

00:21:02.302 --> 00:21:03.512
So that's number one.

00:21:03.512 --> 00:21:06.682
Second for us is drivers
the most loyal drivers,

00:21:06.682 --> 00:21:07.849
the best drivers,

00:21:07.849 --> 00:21:10.644
we now provide them free college education

00:21:12.604 --> 00:21:14.690
through University of Phoenix.

00:21:14.690 --> 00:21:16.483
They can get either a
degree for themselves

00:21:16.483 --> 00:21:17.985
or for a family member.

00:21:17.985 --> 00:21:21.029
Really cool program
that I'm the big fan of.

00:21:21.029 --> 00:21:22.447
We've now built a program

00:21:22.447 --> 00:21:24.950
where we've got the centers of excellence

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:27.077
or Greenlight hubs that essentially

00:21:27.077 --> 00:21:30.539
are customer service centers
for riders and drivers.

00:21:30.539 --> 00:21:34.418
We hire drivers to then
become customer service reps,

00:21:34.418 --> 00:21:37.379
et cetera, if they do
want a full-time job.

00:21:37.379 --> 00:21:38.964
So that's a ladder,

00:21:38.964 --> 00:21:41.466
that moves up to customer
service, et cetera.

00:21:41.466 --> 00:21:45.137
We then take the best of
our Greenlight agents,

00:21:45.137 --> 00:21:47.848
customer service hub agents,

00:21:47.848 --> 00:21:51.560
and we have classes now where
we teach them how to code.

00:21:52.894 --> 00:21:55.272
We have had classes where we teach them

00:21:55.272 --> 00:21:57.941
how to be sales people to
go and sell to restaurants

00:21:57.941 --> 00:21:59.151
for Uber Eats.

00:21:59.151 --> 00:22:02.487
So we're creating our own paths up

00:22:02.487 --> 00:22:04.740
where if you wanna stay a driver,

00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:07.409
you can make more and
you have certain benefits

00:22:07.409 --> 00:22:08.744
and you have educational benefits

00:22:08.744 --> 00:22:12.080
either for your family or
you can enter the Uber system

00:22:12.080 --> 00:22:14.291
in a full-time manner if that's something-

00:22:14.291 --> 00:22:15.625
- That's really interesting.

00:22:15.625 --> 00:22:16.668
So you're not stuck as a driver.

00:22:16.668 --> 00:22:17.502
There is a progression.

00:22:17.502 --> 00:22:18.879
That's really-

00:22:18.879 --> 00:22:20.088
- Yeah, I mean you've gotta
build that progression.

00:22:20.088 --> 00:22:23.050
I think companies talk
about it, we try to do it.

00:22:23.050 --> 00:22:24.885
- That's great.

00:22:24.885 --> 00:22:27.387
You've been in Ukraine
just before coming here.

00:22:27.387 --> 00:22:28.430
For a couple of days.

00:22:30.140 --> 00:22:32.517
Can you just quickly
talk a little bit about

00:22:33.810 --> 00:22:35.437
what you're doing on the ground

00:22:35.437 --> 00:22:38.023
and also what have you learned

00:22:38.023 --> 00:22:39.941
that you want to sort of put in place

00:22:39.941 --> 00:22:43.695
as your planning for other
places around the world

00:22:43.695 --> 00:22:45.322
where disasters happen,

00:22:45.322 --> 00:22:46.782
where you are basically part
of the critical infrastructure

00:22:46.782 --> 00:22:48.033
of the transportation?

00:22:48.033 --> 00:22:48.950
So can you just give us a quick-

00:22:48.950 --> 00:22:50.869
- Absolutely, absolutely.

00:22:50.869 --> 00:22:53.080
It was great being on
the ground with the team.

00:22:53.080 --> 00:22:55.874
We've been operating
in Ukraine since 2016.

00:22:55.874 --> 00:22:59.836
We've got about 25,000 drivers in Ukraine.

00:22:59.836 --> 00:23:02.964
And we first started with using
our transportation services

00:23:02.964 --> 00:23:06.468
to get refugees to safety, get
them to the border of Poland.

00:23:06.468 --> 00:23:11.139
Now we're focused on helping
doctors get to hospitals,

00:23:11.139 --> 00:23:12.724
helping teachers get to schools.

00:23:12.724 --> 00:23:14.601
I met some of those doctors.

00:23:14.601 --> 00:23:15.977
I met some of those teachers.

00:23:15.977 --> 00:23:18.980
The transportation that
we take for granted here,

00:23:18.980 --> 00:23:22.484
they can't take for granted
when they're rockets attacks,

00:23:22.484 --> 00:23:24.277
when there are power outages, et cetera.

00:23:24.277 --> 00:23:29.282
The availability mass
transit is quite spotty.

00:23:30.617 --> 00:23:34.788
We then built a really
interesting program with the UN

00:23:34.788 --> 00:23:39.709
getting food and also
items for winter shelter,

00:23:39.709 --> 00:23:41.086
blankets, et cetera,

00:23:41.086 --> 00:23:43.130
where we built a private label version

00:23:43.130 --> 00:23:46.967
of essentially the Uber
fulfillment infrastructure.

00:23:46.967 --> 00:23:50.095
You don't want to use big
trucks, mark vans, et cetera,

00:23:50.095 --> 00:23:52.264
to get these supplies out to NGOs

00:23:52.264 --> 00:23:56.101
that then get them to real people in need

00:23:56.101 --> 00:23:57.227
who've been displaced.

00:23:58.228 --> 00:24:00.605
So we essentially have
a private label Uber,

00:24:00.605 --> 00:24:04.901
where they can hail an Uber,
it's a private vehicle,

00:24:04.901 --> 00:24:06.653
comes in, picks up the
stuff, or a private truck,

00:24:06.653 --> 00:24:08.697
picks up the stuff, gets them to the NGO,

00:24:09.614 --> 00:24:11.950
they get them out to folks
who've been displaced.

00:24:11.950 --> 00:24:14.703
Again, I got to see one
of those NGOs at work.

00:24:14.703 --> 00:24:19.166
Very Tech savvy, very
entrepreneurial, incredibly organized.

00:24:19.166 --> 00:24:22.335
So for me it was really encouraging

00:24:22.335 --> 00:24:24.296
to see the work on the ground.

00:24:24.296 --> 00:24:25.797
We work with the Ministry of Culture

00:24:25.797 --> 00:24:27.632
to provide transportation,

00:24:27.632 --> 00:24:32.637
to take works of art and get
them to places of safety,

00:24:34.097 --> 00:24:37.392
get them restored so that we can retain

00:24:37.392 --> 00:24:40.228
these incredibly important
pieces of art and culture

00:24:40.228 --> 00:24:41.354
in Ukraine as well.

00:24:41.354 --> 00:24:44.316
So for me, seeing it on the
ground and most importantly,

00:24:44.316 --> 00:24:46.443
supporting the team,

00:24:46.443 --> 00:24:47.652
letting them know that I'm with them

00:24:47.652 --> 00:24:50.489
and also making sure
that I send the message

00:24:50.489 --> 00:24:52.157
to Uber employees

00:24:52.157 --> 00:24:55.410
and hopefully broaders that
we've got to stay with Ukraine.

00:24:55.410 --> 00:25:00.415
I went to Bucha where these
terrible atrocities happen.

00:25:01.958 --> 00:25:06.963
When you see the pit in which
these victims were buried,

00:25:07.714 --> 00:25:10.217
not buried, I mean, throw it into it.

00:25:10.217 --> 00:25:13.929
It just breaks your heart.

00:25:13.929 --> 00:25:17.265
And I think we're talking
about it here, which is great,

00:25:17.265 --> 00:25:19.976
is that we've got to
stand behind your Ukraine.

00:25:19.976 --> 00:25:22.062
No one knows when this war is gonna end.

00:25:22.979 --> 00:25:26.358
And we've got to stay behind Ukraine

00:25:26.358 --> 00:25:27.567
for as long as it takes.

00:25:27.567 --> 00:25:29.653
Uber certainly intends to do so.

00:25:29.653 --> 00:25:32.656
- And what have you learned in terms of,

00:25:32.656 --> 00:25:36.201
in other countries when
unexpected things happen

00:25:36.201 --> 00:25:39.329
particularly quickly in terms of plans

00:25:39.329 --> 00:25:40.372
that can click into place

00:25:40.372 --> 00:25:44.125
to facilitate some of these movement?

00:25:46.127 --> 00:25:50.507
- The really interesting
factor in terms of Uber

00:25:50.507 --> 00:25:54.261
and what makes it interesting
is that it's so unpredictable,

00:25:54.261 --> 00:25:56.555
every single city in which
we operate is different.

00:25:56.555 --> 00:25:58.306
Every single circumstance is different.

00:25:58.306 --> 00:26:00.225
So we don't have a playbook.

00:26:00.225 --> 00:26:02.561
The playbook is, "How can we help?"

00:26:03.436 --> 00:26:04.688
And so with Ukraine,

00:26:04.688 --> 00:26:07.023
it literally started with refugees

00:26:07.023 --> 00:26:08.608
and then it just
organically went to places

00:26:08.608 --> 00:26:09.609
that we can help.

00:26:09.609 --> 00:26:13.029
I'm sitting down with a ton
of partner organizations

00:26:13.029 --> 00:26:15.699
and it's just how can we
help, how can we do more?

00:26:15.699 --> 00:26:19.703
Uber is unique in that
we're a technology company.

00:26:19.703 --> 00:26:22.414
We've got a big scope,
we're in over 70 countries,

00:26:22.414 --> 00:26:24.207
but we're hyper hyperlocal.

00:26:24.207 --> 00:26:25.875
So the playbook is the teams

00:26:25.875 --> 00:26:27.877
working with government officials

00:26:27.877 --> 00:26:30.213
and then just being
entrepreneurial in general

00:26:30.213 --> 00:26:33.008
and trying to help in every
single city in which we operate.

00:26:33.008 --> 00:26:34.134
- Brilliant, thank you.

00:26:35.635 --> 00:26:38.805
I got an email yesterday
from WEF saying that Nadella,

00:26:38.805 --> 00:26:40.932
who was here actually earlier in the week,

00:26:41.808 --> 00:26:43.768
was talking about the AI golden age

00:26:43.768 --> 00:26:45.854
and that it's good for humanity.

00:26:45.854 --> 00:26:46.980
Do you agree with that?

00:26:48.106 --> 00:26:50.859
- Yes, there will be volatility,

00:26:50.859 --> 00:26:52.485
but long term it'll be good for humanity.

00:26:52.485 --> 00:26:54.863
- And what does it mean for your business?

00:26:56.448 --> 00:27:01.036
- It's not the kind of the
Chachi PT, AI, et cetera,

00:27:01.036 --> 00:27:04.539
but we use machine learning
extensively in our business

00:27:04.539 --> 00:27:08.418
in terms of how we deal
with real-world situations.

00:27:08.418 --> 00:27:13.381
If you look at Uber, the
pricing routing, et cetera

00:27:13.673 --> 00:27:15.425
is incredibly dynamic.

00:27:15.425 --> 00:27:19.554
And our system has to,
on a real-time basis,

00:27:19.554 --> 00:27:21.848
adjust to everything happening locally.

00:27:21.848 --> 00:27:23.767
There is no kind of
global model, et cetera.

00:27:23.767 --> 00:27:25.727
But we do have these AI models

00:27:25.727 --> 00:27:29.356
that essentially are learning
behaviors on a local basis.

00:27:29.356 --> 00:27:32.442
So if you look at our pricing
algos, matching algos,

00:27:32.442 --> 00:27:36.071
routing algorithms, fraud
algorithms, et cetera,

00:27:36.071 --> 00:27:40.492
all of it is powered by
larger learning models

00:27:40.492 --> 00:27:43.828
that continue to get better
and better and better.

00:27:43.828 --> 00:27:45.830
So it's a pretty practical use of AI,

00:27:45.830 --> 00:27:49.376
but it's in everything that we do.

00:27:49.376 --> 00:27:51.711
- Okay, and when you answered
the original question,

00:27:51.711 --> 00:27:54.255
you had to think a little bit.

00:27:54.255 --> 00:27:55.090
- It happens sometimes.

00:27:55.090 --> 00:28:00.095
- Yeah, it wasn't a criticism,
it what are the downsides?

00:28:01.513 --> 00:28:04.557
I mean you obviously, there's
obviously a balance in there.

00:28:04.557 --> 00:28:06.810
I mean, what do you see
as the risk, technology?

00:28:06.810 --> 00:28:08.770
- Yeah, I think the
downsides are that, well,

00:28:08.770 --> 00:28:11.231
I guess short term downsides
or pain could be that

00:28:11.231 --> 00:28:12.941
there will be displacement, right?

00:28:12.941 --> 00:28:15.443
So if you think about ChatGPT

00:28:15.443 --> 00:28:19.989
you can imagine restaurant
menu descriptions,

00:28:19.989 --> 00:28:23.827
restaurant descriptions
being written by AI,

00:28:23.827 --> 00:28:27.706
the copy in terms of
email and communications

00:28:27.706 --> 00:28:29.666
that we send to our customers,

00:28:29.666 --> 00:28:33.169
being written by AI and being personalized

00:28:33.169 --> 00:28:34.462
on a one-to-one basis.

00:28:34.462 --> 00:28:36.965
The use cases are pretty
powerful right now.

00:28:36.965 --> 00:28:39.718
When we send in email to you

00:28:39.718 --> 00:28:43.012
thanking you for being an Uber customer,

00:28:43.012 --> 00:28:44.347
it's a standard email.

00:28:44.347 --> 00:28:45.974
It will be an email to you

00:28:45.974 --> 00:28:47.600
that will be personalized, et cetera.

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:50.228
That standard email is run by a person.

00:28:50.228 --> 00:28:54.774
So the applications are going to initially

00:28:56.067 --> 00:28:58.570
put some people out of jobs.

00:28:58.570 --> 00:29:01.489
But what happens in our economy
always on a long-term basis

00:29:01.489 --> 00:29:03.783
than those people find other use cases

00:29:03.783 --> 00:29:06.119
or else you may go from a copywriter

00:29:06.119 --> 00:29:11.124
to a ChatGPT user who is
responsible for the nature

00:29:14.169 --> 00:29:17.756
and the feel of these emails

00:29:17.756 --> 00:29:20.341
that ultimately are written
by ChatGPT, et cetera.

00:29:20.341 --> 00:29:23.595
So that adjustment
period can be difficult,

00:29:23.595 --> 00:29:25.305
can be scary, et cetera.

00:29:25.305 --> 00:29:28.224
But I think the end state's
gonna be a good one.

00:29:28.224 --> 00:29:30.685
- We have time for, oh wow, okay.

00:29:30.685 --> 00:29:32.771
(audience laughing)

00:29:32.771 --> 00:29:36.983
In the front, questions was my last word,

00:29:36.983 --> 00:29:39.194
which I didn't get to, but go for it.

00:29:39.194 --> 00:29:40.987
- [Audience Member 1] Thank you Thorold,

00:29:40.987 --> 00:29:43.239
Dara, you came into Uber

00:29:43.239 --> 00:29:48.244
in a fairly tumultuous
public cultural situation.

00:29:48.369 --> 00:29:49.829
My curiosity to you is,

00:29:49.829 --> 00:29:52.373
as you looked at the culture of Uber,

00:29:52.373 --> 00:29:55.835
what were the behaviors
that you wanted to retain?

00:29:55.835 --> 00:29:57.921
What were the ones that
you wanted to change?

00:29:57.921 --> 00:30:01.466
And I'm particularly interested
in your executive team

00:30:01.466 --> 00:30:04.385
and how you leverage that executive team

00:30:04.385 --> 00:30:05.804
to change the culture of Uber.

00:30:05.804 --> 00:30:06.638
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:30:06.638 --> 00:30:07.555
It's a great question.

00:30:08.848 --> 00:30:12.894
The culture that I want to
retain at Uber at the time was

00:30:12.894 --> 00:30:15.730
this is incredible entrepreneurial energy,

00:30:15.730 --> 00:30:18.900
innovative, go get them Culture

00:30:19.818 --> 00:30:22.278
that was always thinking about

00:30:22.278 --> 00:30:25.573
what's the newest best way of operating.

00:30:25.573 --> 00:30:27.075
It's incredible rally culture.

00:30:27.075 --> 00:30:28.868
It's like, "Let's go,

00:30:28.868 --> 00:30:31.120
we're not gonna wait for
a second, et cetera."

00:30:31.120 --> 00:30:32.872
That that culture,

00:30:32.872 --> 00:30:36.084
we've kept the majority of that culture

00:30:36.084 --> 00:30:37.919
and that culture is like
second to none, I love it.

00:30:37.919 --> 00:30:41.381
It is so much fun because people
really rally at the company

00:30:44.300 --> 00:30:47.887
that culture was used to
be the ultimate disruptor.

00:30:48.972 --> 00:30:52.600
In terms of disrupting the
transportation system, et cetera.

00:30:52.600 --> 00:30:55.645
At some point the disruptor
became an incumbent

00:30:56.688 --> 00:31:00.692
and where the company
failed was to understand

00:31:00.692 --> 00:31:03.820
the responsibilities that
come with incumbency.

00:31:03.820 --> 00:31:05.864
And if you are acting like a disruptor,

00:31:05.864 --> 00:31:07.490
but you're really an incumbent,

00:31:07.490 --> 00:31:10.618
you kind of look like a jerk, right?

00:31:11.661 --> 00:31:14.622
And you're not taking a
responsibility seriously, et cetera.

00:31:14.622 --> 00:31:17.625
I don't think it's because
there's anyone who was bad there,

00:31:17.625 --> 00:31:19.669
but things were moving so quickly

00:31:19.669 --> 00:31:21.671
that there wasn't the time
to kind of reflect and say,

00:31:21.671 --> 00:31:23.923
"Well, what are responsibilities?"

00:31:23.923 --> 00:31:26.551
So the changes that I made
in terms of management

00:31:26.551 --> 00:31:31.347
actually were on the executive team

00:31:31.347 --> 00:31:34.100
that was more responsible
for some of these factors.

00:31:34.100 --> 00:31:37.478
I brought a new CFO, Nelson Chai,

00:31:37.478 --> 00:31:41.482
I brought in new general counsel,
new head of HR, et cetera.

00:31:41.482 --> 00:31:44.986
And then at the same
time the operating leads

00:31:44.986 --> 00:31:47.322
of the company who are
kind of doing the real work

00:31:47.322 --> 00:31:50.742
in terms of operations, the
head of mobility operations,

00:31:50.742 --> 00:31:55.747
delivery operations, or
head of comms and marketing,

00:31:56.956 --> 00:31:58.166
Jill Hazelbaker who's here,

00:31:58.166 --> 00:32:02.086
like these were stars who were there,

00:32:02.086 --> 00:32:03.796
who were at Uber before me

00:32:03.796 --> 00:32:06.466
and hopefully will be at Uber after me.

00:32:06.466 --> 00:32:08.801
So I think what I'm trying
to achieve with Uber

00:32:08.801 --> 00:32:11.387
is a combination of
the newest and the old,

00:32:11.387 --> 00:32:13.890
which is keep that
entrepreneurial energy and edge

00:32:14.724 --> 00:32:17.101
and the ability to move incredibly fast,

00:32:17.101 --> 00:32:21.856
but recognize the enormous
responsibility that we have

00:32:21.856 --> 00:32:26.819
and the dialogue that, can slow us down.

00:32:27.111 --> 00:32:31.157
Which we're an impatient group
but is very, very necessary

00:32:31.157 --> 00:32:34.619
to make sure that ultimately when we act,

00:32:34.619 --> 00:32:36.412
we're acting with the right intent.

00:32:36.412 --> 00:32:38.247
And the output is that
we're doing the right thing

00:32:38.247 --> 00:32:39.540
as a company.

00:32:39.540 --> 00:32:42.293
- There's another, I'll go
there, there and then there.

00:32:44.504 --> 00:32:45.713
- [Audience Member 2] Thank you.

00:32:45.713 --> 00:32:49.258
So Uber has committed
to be net zero by 2040.

00:32:49.258 --> 00:32:50.718
Can you get there earlier?

00:32:51.594 --> 00:32:53.096
- We can in certain places, right?

00:32:53.096 --> 00:32:57.058
So we have committed
to be net zero by 2030

00:32:57.058 --> 00:32:59.894
in the US, Canada and Europe.

00:32:59.894 --> 00:33:03.439
In London, for example, the target's 2025

00:33:03.439 --> 00:33:06.192
and we are making progress.

00:33:06.192 --> 00:33:08.695
For example, California now

00:33:08.695 --> 00:33:12.699
we're at about 10%
kilometer miles being EVs,

00:33:12.699 --> 00:33:13.658
which is pretty awesome.

00:33:13.658 --> 00:33:15.827
Lots of Teslas in California.

00:33:15.827 --> 00:33:19.205
In Europe it's about 8% kilometer miles

00:33:19.205 --> 00:33:23.626
and about five times the percentage

00:33:23.626 --> 00:33:27.046
of generally what you see
kilometer miles in Europe.

00:33:27.046 --> 00:33:31.134
And I think that shifting the
Uber driver over drive an EV

00:33:32.051 --> 00:33:34.887
is so much more impactful
than the average driver

00:33:34.887 --> 00:33:36.806
because the Uber driver's driving

00:33:36.806 --> 00:33:39.267
five to six to seven times,

00:33:39.267 --> 00:33:41.352
the number of miles and
then the average drivers.

00:33:41.352 --> 00:33:43.688
So this is a very, very
targeted group of people

00:33:43.688 --> 00:33:46.441
that we should switch over
to EV as soon as possible.

00:33:46.441 --> 00:33:50.069
The biggest blocker I talked
about are affordable EVs

00:33:50.069 --> 00:33:54.240
and also charging
infrastructure within cities.

00:33:54.240 --> 00:33:57.827
Most cities are putting
charging infrastructure

00:33:57.827 --> 00:33:59.871
in the center of cities.

00:33:59.871 --> 00:34:02.415
That's where wealthy people tend to live.

00:34:02.415 --> 00:34:05.918
But they're not putting EV infrastructure

00:34:05.918 --> 00:34:07.879
where many of our drivers live

00:34:07.879 --> 00:34:09.797
and many of our drivers need it,

00:34:09.797 --> 00:34:11.549
which is in some of the outskirts

00:34:11.549 --> 00:34:13.843
in other neighboring areas of the city.

00:34:13.843 --> 00:34:16.846
We are working with cities
to give them the data

00:34:16.846 --> 00:34:17.722
to provide them with,

00:34:17.722 --> 00:34:19.182
"Hey, here are the different areas

00:34:19.182 --> 00:34:21.350
where you should be putting
charging infrastructure."

00:34:21.350 --> 00:34:24.353
And we're working both
with private and public

00:34:24.353 --> 00:34:29.358
to help shape the plans in terms
of charging infrastructure.

00:34:29.984 --> 00:34:32.070
It's going in the right direction,

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:33.613
but it's gonna take a lot of effort

00:34:33.613 --> 00:34:35.615
from a lot of people who get there.

00:34:35.615 --> 00:34:36.908
Sure go ahead.

00:34:38.242 --> 00:34:39.744
- [Philippe] Yes, I'm Philippe Monnier.

00:34:39.744 --> 00:34:41.788
I representative Swiss technology provider

00:34:41.788 --> 00:34:45.333
specialized in automotive, named WayRay,

00:34:45.333 --> 00:34:48.252
you said before that you're talking to OEM

00:34:48.252 --> 00:34:51.464
in order to have customized car,

00:34:51.464 --> 00:34:55.718
especially for you ridesharing services.

00:34:55.718 --> 00:34:57.720
If it is not secret,

00:34:57.720 --> 00:35:00.139
can you share to which OEM you're talking,

00:35:00.139 --> 00:35:05.144
which specific characteristic
those car will have?

00:35:06.437 --> 00:35:10.358
- I will give the very unsanitary
answer of it is secret.

00:35:10.358 --> 00:35:11.526
(Thorold laughs)

00:35:11.526 --> 00:35:12.443
I'm sorry.

00:35:12.443 --> 00:35:13.486
- [Philippe] Characteristics.

00:35:15.196 --> 00:35:19.450
- The characteristics for ridesharing,

00:35:19.450 --> 00:35:22.453
one is the passenger area.

00:35:22.453 --> 00:35:25.248
Sometimes passengers facing each other.

00:35:25.248 --> 00:35:28.000
I do think that top speeds, for example,

00:35:28.000 --> 00:35:31.129
top speeds that many cars have

00:35:31.129 --> 00:35:33.256
are not necessary for city driving

00:35:33.256 --> 00:35:35.508
that's associated with ride share.

00:35:35.508 --> 00:35:38.594
We're also talking about
purpose built vehicles

00:35:38.594 --> 00:35:40.012
for delivery, right?

00:35:40.012 --> 00:35:42.140
You can imagine smaller
vehicles, two-wheelers,

00:35:42.140 --> 00:35:43.975
three wheelers that have trunk space

00:35:43.975 --> 00:35:46.352
that that can get through traffic easier

00:35:46.352 --> 00:35:50.189
and have a much smaller footprint

00:35:50.189 --> 00:35:53.359
both in terms of environmental
but also traffic footprint

00:35:53.359 --> 00:35:56.529
than let's say a car to
go and deliver groceries.

00:35:56.529 --> 00:35:58.156
So those are the various
area we're looking at.

00:35:58.156 --> 00:36:00.533
- So you're likely to
be more of a black cab

00:36:00.533 --> 00:36:02.076
facing each other model, isn't it?

00:36:02.076 --> 00:36:03.995
- That's certainly a model
that we're looking at,

00:36:03.995 --> 00:36:06.664
but part of it is just what
are the capabilities, right?

00:36:06.664 --> 00:36:09.083
You're not gonna have in many cities,

00:36:09.083 --> 00:36:10.543
quite as much highway driving

00:36:11.419 --> 00:36:12.795
than you do in the US et cetera.

00:36:12.795 --> 00:36:15.298
That can reduce the specs
and if you reduce the specs,

00:36:15.298 --> 00:36:16.841
you can reduce the ultimate cost.

00:36:16.841 --> 00:36:20.595
- Final question, and I know
we don't go but the gentleman.

00:36:20.595 --> 00:36:22.889
- [Audience Member 3] As
it would take some time

00:36:22.889 --> 00:36:24.015
to have more Electric cars,

00:36:24.015 --> 00:36:24.974
would you consider to have an option

00:36:24.974 --> 00:36:27.435
allow the customer who
can not take Electric car

00:36:27.435 --> 00:36:29.061
and then just like, would you like to tip

00:36:29.061 --> 00:36:31.981
or would you like to offset
your carbon footprint

00:36:31.981 --> 00:36:33.524
after they take the journey?

00:36:33.524 --> 00:36:34.817
- So I think understanding your question,

00:36:34.817 --> 00:36:39.488
we have the option for
riders now to take Uber Green

00:36:39.488 --> 00:36:42.450
and to specifically request Electric cars.

00:36:42.450 --> 00:36:44.702
And what we've seen from customers

00:36:44.702 --> 00:36:47.747
is that customers generally
don't want to pay a premium

00:36:47.747 --> 00:36:49.999
in terms of dollars for an Electric car,

00:36:49.999 --> 00:36:53.044
but they are willing to pay
a premium in terms of time.

00:36:53.044 --> 00:36:56.589
So if the average time to get a ride

00:36:56.589 --> 00:36:59.133
is four minutes in market,
five minutes in market,

00:36:59.133 --> 00:37:02.470
there's a segment of customers
who will wait 10 minutes

00:37:02.470 --> 00:37:04.597
to get an Electric car,

00:37:05.514 --> 00:37:08.476
customers tip better for Electric cars.

00:37:08.476 --> 00:37:12.230
It just kind of works that
way, which is pretty cool.

00:37:12.230 --> 00:37:15.024
We are looking at offsets,

00:37:15.024 --> 00:37:17.985
but honestly we're spending more time

00:37:17.985 --> 00:37:20.404
just changing over the fleet, right?

00:37:20.404 --> 00:37:23.199
So offsets is certainly a
characteristic we'll look at,

00:37:23.199 --> 00:37:25.159
but I think just spending the energy

00:37:25.159 --> 00:37:27.161
on changing over the fleet

00:37:27.161 --> 00:37:29.330
is the highest impact activity of the day.

